        "From the form: first and last name, date of birth, sex, nationality, the country you live in and your city, email, telephone and the form's language. We use them to run the event: places and the waiting list, the messages about your registration and your race number (name and email); age and sex category and whether a minor must be registered by a parent (date of birth and sex); to reach you on race day if needed (telephone); to know where participants come from (nationality, country and city). Basis: art. 6(1)(b) GDPR — performing the participation agreement. The required ones are marked on the form: without them you cannot register. Your confirmed email address identifies you (no account or password); we also keep it in canonical form, so nobody takes two places. You may also register someone else — a family — on the same address, up to the limit the club sets: each person stays a separate registration, with their own data above, their own declaration and their own place. If you register someone else on your address, do so with their knowledge and agreement; we receive their details from you (art. 14(2)(f) GDPR). The messages about them — the confirmation, the declaration (the signed copy, with their identity document's series and number), the reminders — go to your address in their name; the first one says where we got the details and links to this notice (art. 14(3)(b) GDPR). Show them these messages. The consents — for the health note, for appearing on the public list and for Strava and Instagram — can be given only by the person themselves; for a minor, by the parent or guardian. Whoever registers another adult cannot give them on that adult's behalf, so we do not ask for them on that registration; the declaration, with the statement about health, is signed by the person themselves.",
        "Your emergency contact: we keep the name and telephone number you give us only to call that person if something happens to you at the event — the legitimate interest of you and the club in telling someone close (art. 6(1)(f) GDPR) and, in an emergency, vital interests (art. 6(1)(d)). Tell them you gave us their details; the rights in section 8 are theirs too. Only the club's organisers and administrators see them, on the registration's page and the event's emergency sheet, and we delete them seven days after the event.",
